  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWhile scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms continue to\ntraverse the mountains early this morning beneath a mid/upper-\nlevel short wave trough and ahead of a cold front crossing the\nmiddle Ohio Valley, the worst of the weather is over, the Severe\nThunderstorm Watches were canceled or allowed to expire, and \nthe Flood Watch for Flash Flooding has been allowed to expire.\n\nHowever, flooding, and stream and river rises, continue across \nthe northern tier of the county warning area, with some of the \nsmaller stems already in flood, and three larger stems warned \non where there are gauges forecast to exceed flood stage. The \nthree larger stems were the West Fork, Tygart and Buchanan. Of \nnote, Major flooding was already occurring at Buckhannon on the\nBuckhannon, and was forecast to occur at Philippi on the Tygart\nValley and Clarksburg on the West Fork, this afternoon.\n\nOn the Severe end, storm surveys may be needed today for \npossible tornadic damage Tuesday.\n\nShowers and any thunderstorms exit the mountains early this \nmorning, except the far southern mountains, with showers likely\nto linger in southwest Virginia today, with thunderstorms \nremaining possible. Otherwise, areas of low clouds and fog \nthrough daybreak will give way to some sunshine for the balance \nof the area today, with a more comfortable afternoon as cooler \nand drier air caps lowland temperatures around 80 degrees.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure building into the area beneath mid/upper-level \nnorthwest flow provides cooler and less humid weather for the \nbalance of the work week. Temperatures will bottom out in the \n50s area-wide Thursday morning, and may fall just shy of 80 for \nhighs across much of the lowlands Thursday. Temperatures will \nthen start a slow moderating trend Thursday nigh and Friday. \nRiver valley fog, some dense, can be expected Thursday and \nFriday mornings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nWhile the long wave trough in the east and ridge in the west\npersist, a pair of short wave troughs moving through the \nmid/upper-level northwest flow may bring showers, and afternoon \nand evening thunderstorms, to areas in and near the mountains, \nFriday afternoon through Saturday.\n\nThen, a slight eastward shift and deamplification of the pattern\nmay get us closer to the ring of fire, or higher height edge of\nthe mid/upper-level northwest flow, in which convective\ncomplexes may sail southeast across the area, late Sunday or\nSunday night through Monday. A slight shift back to the west and\nreamplification of the pattern may allow a cold to cross by\nmidweek, with another round of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nTemperatures moderate to at least normal by the end of the\nweekend, and then guidance diverges on temperatures during the\nnext work week, which could certainly be impacted by convective\ncomplexes.",
